  "evidence": "- TEXT_G: Leave the EU to restore sovereignty and reassert supremacy of UK law; near-zero net migration with points-based permits, tighter family reunion, Britishness tests, safe-third-country rule, and readiness to amend human rights law; tougher law and order with stronger magistrates, credible sentencing, more prison capacity, and protection for homeowners; uphold Parliament, monarchy, common law, independent House of Lords; repeal the Human Rights Act and the Hunting Act; emphasis on national interest in defence and leaving EU defence structures.\n- TEXT_D: Strong internationalism and support for the EU Constitution and potential euro entry; civil liberties focus (repeal control orders, prosecute terror in mainstream courts), Single Equality Act and hate-crime units; fairer, faster asylum and allowing asylum seekers to work; expansive green agenda (carbon tax, renewables target, congestion charging, road pricing, zero-waste, oppose new SE airports); democratic reforms (STV, votes at 16, elected second chamber).",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile leans TAN (0.60), prioritising order, tradition, national identity, and sovereignty. TEXT_G argues primarily from those TAN commitments (sovereignty over the EU, restrictive immigration, traditional institutions, tougher sentencing), while TEXT_D argues from GAL commitments (internationalism, civil liberties, equality, environmentalism), making TEXT_G closer to the profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
